\section{Dissipation and Interface Misorientation in Phase-Field Plasticity}\label{30005836}

\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$; the standard number fields have their usual meanings. All additional conventions are stated locally.


A phase-field variable describes a diffuse interface. Stored energy is recoverable from the state, whereas dissipation is irreversible loss during evolution. Crystal misorientation compares local lattice orientations; sharp-interface calibration compares diffuse-interface predictions with limiting interface motion. Energetic degeneracy means different microstructures have equal energy.

\paragraph{Catalogue wording retained for traceability.}
How can energy storage be separated from dissipation in plasticity, crystal misorientation represented at phase-field interfaces, sharp-interface processes calibrated, dissipation in discrete phase transformations quantified, and energetically degenerate microstructures distinguished computationally?

\paragraph{Scope and source qualification.}This is a bundled set of modelling and computational questions, not one already specified phase-field PDE.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Separate storage and dissipation, represent interface misorientation, calibrate sharp-interface limits, quantify transformation losses and distinguish equally energetic microstructures.

\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Derived an exact phase-field energy-loss identity and tested orientation-symmetry invariance, exposing nonuniqueness of kinetics from stored energy.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the full supplied definition, catalogue wording, source qualifications and listed references for 30005836. No fresh status or novelty search was performed. The argument below proves its auxiliary statements directly and does not import an unverified external theorem.

\paragraph{Separating state energy from kinetic loss in a test model.} On a periodic interval let \(\phi\) be a smooth scalar phase field and \(W\) a differentiable double-well potential. For \(\epsilon>0\) define
\[
 E_\epsilon(\phi)=\int\left(\frac\epsilon2|\phi_x|^2+\frac1\epsilon W(\phi)\right)dx,
 \quad \mu_\phi=-\epsilon\phi_{xx}+\epsilon^{-1}W'(\phi),
 \quad \phi_t=-M\mu_\phi,
\]
with constant mobility \(M>0\). Integration by parts yields
\[
 \frac{dE_\epsilon}{dt}=\int\mu_\phi\phi_tdx
 =-M\int\mu_\phi^2dx=-M^{-1}\int\phi_t^2dx.
\]
Stationary interfaces are independent of \(M\), but their time-dependent relaxation is not. Equal stored energy therefore cannot determine rates of dissipation.
\paragraph{Misorientation and degeneracy checks.} A scalar phase field alone contains no lattice orientation. Even a planar orientation angle \(\theta\) is defined modulo the crystal symmetry: for an \(n\)-fold rotational symmetry, the mismatch magnitude must compare \(\theta_1-\theta_2\) modulo \(2\pi/n\). The function \(\min_{k\in\mathbb Z}|\theta_1-\theta_2-2\pi k/n|\) passes that invariance test, while the raw angle difference fails it. This distance is nonsmooth at equivalent choices and is not by itself a calibrated interface energy.
\paragraph{Remaining coupled task.} The identity provides a consistency test for storage and loss, not a derivation of plastic slip or discrete transformation dissipation. A calibrated misorientation law, a sharp-interface evolution limit, and observables distinguishing energetically degenerate microstructures remain missing. In particular, the first unresolved step is a constitutive energy and dissipation pair linked to the source discrete physics; arbitrarily choosing \(W\) and \(M\) cannot answer the bundled modelling questions in [S2].

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
Checked periodic integration by parts, positive mobility and dissipation sign, orientation equivalence and the distinction between stationary energy and dynamics. Model self-audit only; no independent human mathematical verification. Applied contribution prompt A before drafting and B afterwards. Checked periodic integration by parts, positive mobility and dissipation sign, orientation equivalence and the distinction between stationary energy and dynamics. First remaining gap: Derive a constitutive energy and dissipation pair from the source microscopic physics with calibrated misorientation and sharp-interface behavior. The inherited source status is preserved; original-target completion remains zero.

Coordinator checked the phase-field energy dissipation identity and crystal-symmetry invariance of the proposed angle distance. This additional check was model-assisted, not independent human verification.

\paragraph{Tools, process and contributions.}
Python 3 and local file tools used to read definitions and serialize manuscripts. Calculations are symbolic reasoning; no numerical experiment or theorem prover. Contribution prompt A was followed by prompt B. GPT-6 Astra with ultra reasoning drafted the research and performed its stated self-audit; any additional coordinator contribution is identified in the verification and audit. A GPT-6-based Codex coordinator assembled the manuscript and checked repository integration; its exact variant and reasoning configuration were not exposed. No human mathematical contribution, independent human proof check or formal proof certification is supplied. Complete derivations and the consulted source references are included here; private reasoning transcripts are not published.

\paragraph{Final status and first remaining gap.}
